WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.860
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:11.120
Today we are going to get a taste of how insanely
quick progress is in machine learning research.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:17.960
In March of 2020, a paper appeared that goes
by the name Neural Radiance Fields, NERF in

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:18.960
short.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.500
With this technique, we could take a bunch
of input photos, get a neural network to learn

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:29.109
them, and then, synthesize new, previously
unseen views of not just the materials in

00:00:29.109 --> 00:00:32.800
the scene, but the entire scene itself.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.860
And here, we are talking not only digital
environments, but also, real scenes as well!

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:44.860
Just to make sure, once again, it can learn
and reproduce entire real-world scenes from

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:48.390
only a few views by using neural networks.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:51.700
However, of course, NERF had its limitations.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:57.350
For instance, in many cases, it had trouble
with scenes with variable lighting conditions

00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:59.870
and lots of occluders.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:06.329
And to my delight, only 5 months later, in
August of 2020, a followup paper appeared

00:01:06.329 --> 00:01:11.289
by the name NERF in the Wild, or NERF-W in
short.

00:01:11.289 --> 00:01:16.899
Its speciality was tourist attractions that
a lot of people take photos of, and we then

00:01:16.899 --> 00:01:22.010
have a collection of photos taken during a
different time of the day, and of course,

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:23.600
with a lot of people around.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.899
And, lots of people, of course means, lots
of occlusions.

00:01:27.899 --> 00:01:33.960
NERF-W improved the original algorithm to
excel more in cases like this.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:40.189
And we are still not done yet, because get
this, only three months later, on 2020 November

00:01:40.189 --> 00:01:46.990
25th, another followup paper appeared by the
name Deformable Neural Radiance Fields.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:47.999
D-NERF.

00:01:47.999 --> 00:01:53.400
The goal here is to take a selfie video, and
turn it into a portrait that we can rotate

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.299
around freely.

00:01:55.299 --> 00:02:00.560
This is something that the authors call a
nerfie.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:05.380
If we take the original NERF technique to
perform this, we see that it does not do well

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.529
at all with moving things.

00:02:07.529 --> 00:02:11.610
And here is where the deformable part of the
name comes into play.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:17.250
And now, hold on to your papers and marvel
at the results of the new D-NERF technique.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.420
A clean reconstruction.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:24.720
We indeed get a nice portrait that we can
rotate around freely and all of the previous

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.920
NERF artifacts are gone.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:37.220
It performs well even on difficult cases with
beards, all kinds of hairstyles, and more.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:43.410
And now, hold on to your papers, because glasses
work too, and not only that, but it even computes

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:48.070
the proper reflection and refraction off of
the lens.

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:51.470
And this is just the start of a deluge of
new features.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:58.080
For instance, we can even zoom out and capture
the whole body of the test subject.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:06.030
Furthermore, it is not limited to people,
it also works on dogs too, although in this

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:12.380
case, we will have to settle with a lower
the resolution output.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:19.460
It can pull off the iconic dolly zoom effect
really well.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:29.510
And, amusingly, we can even perform a nerfception,
which is recording ourselves as we record

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:30.650
ourselves.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:36.430
I hope that now you have a good feel of the
pace of progress in machine learning research,

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:39.150
which is absolutely incredible.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:43.290
So much progress in just 9 months of research.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:44.460
My goodness.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:04:32.650
What a time to be alive!

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:40.900
Thanks 
for watching and for your generous support,

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:42.530
and I'll see you next time!

